/K [6]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (From a strategic standpoint, choice of forum becomes critical. While jurisdictional errors justify invoking writ jurisdiction, repeated remands may dilute the benefit of such challenge. In appropriate cases, pursuing the statutory appellate route could lead to a more conclusive outcome. Further, applying the doctrine of election, once the department chooses an incorrect statutory route, it should ordinarily be bound by that choice, and the proceedings ought to have been given quietus rather than revival through remand.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (The key takeaway is that jurisdictional defects and defective SCNs should not be lightly cured through remand, as doing so risks converting a fatal illegality into a curable irregularity, contrary to settled principles of law.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
